-With `DIGITIZER_MOUSE_EDGE_SWIPE_ENABLE`, two fingers whose midpoint starts within `DIGITIZER_EDGE_SWIPE_ZONE` of the left or right edge (for example, placed one above the other along the edge) send nothing while they move mostly inwards. Once the midpoint has moved `DIGITIZER_EDGE_SWIPE_DISTANCE` inwards, `DIGITIZER_EDGE_SWIPE_BACK_KC` (from the left edge) or `DIGITIZER_EDGE_SWIPE_FORWARD_KC` (from the right edge) is sent with `tap_code16()`, which is back and forward in macOS browsers by default. The rest of the gesture is then ignored until the fingers lift or change. If the fingers instead pinch, move outwards, move more vertically than inwards once they travelled `DIGITIZER_TWO_FINGER_DECISION_DISTANCE`, or move `DIGITIZER_EDGE_SWIPE_THRESHOLD` vertically, the gesture scrolls or pinches as usual and the motion made so far is not lost. Gestures that start outside the edge zones are not affected. The edges are measured in digitizer coordinates, so `digitizer_set_scale()` below 100% (without `DIGITIZER_MOUSE_CPI_POINTER_ONLY`) moves them inwards.
+With `DIGITIZER_MOUSE_EDGE_SWIPE_ENABLE`, two fingers whose midpoint starts within `DIGITIZER_EDGE_SWIPE_ZONE` of the left or right edge (for example, placed one above the other along the edge) send nothing while they move mostly inwards. Once the midpoint has moved `DIGITIZER_EDGE_SWIPE_DISTANCE` inwards, `DIGITIZER_EDGE_SWIPE_BACK_KC` (from the left edge) or `DIGITIZER_EDGE_SWIPE_FORWARD_KC` (from the right edge) is sent with `tap_code16()`, which is back and forward in macOS browsers by default. The rest of the gesture is then ignored until the fingers lift or change. If the fingers instead pinch, move outwards, move more vertically than inwards once they travelled `DIGITIZER_TWO_FINGER_DECISION_DISTANCE`, or move `DIGITIZER_EDGE_SWIPE_THRESHOLD` vertically, the gesture scrolls or pinches as usual and the motion made so far is not lost. Gestures that start outside the edge zones are not affected by edge swipes. The edges are measured in digitizer coordinates, so `digitizer_set_scale()` below 100% (without `DIGITIZER_MOUSE_CPI_POINTER_ONLY`) moves them inwards.
+
+With `DIGITIZER_MOUSE_FLICK_ENABLE`, two fingers that start outside the edge zones and move the same way, mostly horizontally, can flick, like swiping between pages on a Mac trackpad. Once the fingers travelled `DIGITIZER_TWO_FINGER_DECISION_DISTANCE`, horizontal motion (vertical travel of the midpoint below `DIGITIZER_FLICK_THRESHOLD` and at most half of its horizontal travel) sends nothing until either the midpoint moved `DIGITIZER_FLICK_DISTANCE` horizontally within `DIGITIZER_FLICK_TIMEOUT` ms, which sends `DIGITIZER_FLICK_RIGHT_KC` (fingers moving right) or `DIGITIZER_FLICK_LEFT_KC` (fingers moving left) once with `tap_code16()` and ignores the rest of the gesture until the fingers lift or change, or the timeout passes, which scrolls and sends the motion made so far without loss. The timeout counts from when either finger moved `DIGITIZER_FLICK_REST_DISTANCE`, so resting the fingers first does not use it up. Vertical or diagonal motion scrolls at once, a pinch still pinches, and edge swipes keep priority inside the edge zones. Without `DIGITIZER_MOUSE_PINCH_ENABLE`, two fingers also wait for `DIGITIZER_TWO_FINGER_DECISION_DISTANCE` before scrolling. If flicks fire while scrolling, raise `DIGITIZER_FLICK_DISTANCE` or lower `DIGITIZER_FLICK_TIMEOUT`.
 
